<h2>Indian guar gum for export</h2>
<p>India supplies the large majority of the world's guar gum, and Rajasthan — specifically the Bikaner, Jodhpur and Hanumangarh belt — is where most of that comes from. We supply both refined-split and treated/pulverized (powder) guar gum, tested and graded by viscosity and mesh size to your specification.</p>

<p>Guar is a monsoon (kharif) crop, harvested September to November, but because processed guar gum stores well in sealed packaging, we supply it year-round from stored seed stock — one of the more schedule-flexible products in our range.</p>

<h2>Guar gum export policy and EU documentation</h2>
<p>Guar gum export is Free under Indian policy — India's dominance of world supply means this has never been a constrained product. There is one specific documentation requirement worth knowing about if you're buying for the EU: following a 2012 industry-wide episode involving pentachlorophenol (PCP) contamination traces found in some shipments, EU-bound guar gum for food, feed or human-consumption use now requires an analytical report testing for PCP, endorsed by SHEFEXIL (the Shellac &amp; Forest Products Export Promotion Council, Kolkata). We arrange this as standard documentation on EU orders — it isn't a barrier, just a step to plan for in your timeline.</p>

<h2>How should I specify guar gum?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Grade.</strong> Food/pharma-grade for thickening and stabilizing applications, or industrial-grade for oilfield and technical uses — the end use changes the spec.</li>
  <li><strong>Viscosity.</strong> Typically 3000–6000 cps for food grade, 5000+ cps for industrial — tested per batch and shown on the certificate of analysis.</li>
  <li><strong>Mesh size.</strong> 80 through 300 mesh — finer mesh hydrates faster, which matters more for some industrial applications than others.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian guar gum?</h2>
<p>The USA is the largest market, with Russia, Germany and China also significant — a mix of food/pharma thickener demand and industrial (fracturing-fluid) demand. Canada, Australia, the Netherlands, Italy, Brazil and Indonesia round out a genuinely global buyer base.</p>

